{"name":"Safflower Kite","hex":"#9a493f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"safflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cultivated thistle-like plant, Carthamus tinctorius, family Asteraceae, now grown mainly for its oil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/safflower"},{"term":"kite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bird of prey of the family Accipitridae.","Any bird of the subfamily Milvinae, with long wings and weak legs, feeding mostly on carrion and spending long periods soaring; specifically, the red kite (Milvus milvus) and the black kite (Milvus migrans)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (something) to move upwards rapidly like a toy kite; also  to cause (something, such as costs) to increase rapidly.","To tamper with a document or record by increasing the quantity of something beyond its proper amount so that the difference may be unlawfully retained; in particular, to alter a medical prescription for this purpose by increasing the number of pills or other items."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Safflower","blocks":[{"text":"Safflower, also false saffron, is a highly branched, herbaceous, thistle-like annual plant in the family Asteraceae. It is one of the world's oldest crops; today, it is commercially cultivated for vegetable oil extracted from the seeds. Plants are 30 to 150 cm tall with globular flower heads having yellow, orange, or red flowers. Each branch will usually have from one to five flower heads containing 15 to 20 seeds per head. Safflower is native to arid environments having seasonal rain. It grows a deep taproot which enables it to thrive in such environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Safflower.jpg/330px-Safflower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safflower"},{"title":"Kite","blocks":[{"text":"A kite is a tethered heavier-than-air craft with wing surfaces that react against the air to create lift and drag forces. A kite consists of wings, tethers and anchors. Kites often have a bridle and tail to guide the face of the kite so the wind can lift it. Some kite designs do not need a bridle; box kites can have a single attachment point. A kite may have fixed or moving anchors that can balance the kite. The name is derived from the kite, the hovering bird of prey. There are several kite shapes, including the popular delta kite and diamond kite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Kitesflying.jpg/330px-Kitesflying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kite"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carthamus tinctorius","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Carthamus_tinctorius0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutput.png/500px-Saffloweroutput.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutput.png/1280px-Saffloweroutput.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Saffloweroutpu